(The Pythagorean theorem describes right-angled triangles.) Tj T*
(In a right-angled triangle the longest side is called the hypotenuse.) Tj T*
(The theorem states that a^2 + b^2 = c^2, where c is the hypotenuse.) Tj T*
(The square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides.) Tj T*
(For example, a triangle with sides 3 and 4 has a hypotenuse of length 5.) Tj T*
(This is because 3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25, and the square root of 25 is 5.) Tj T*
(Another example is the triangle with sides 5, 12 and 13.) Tj T*
(Builders use the theorem to check that the corners of walls are square.) Tj T*
(The theorem is named after the Greek mathematician Pythagoras.) Tj T*
(It only works for right-angled triangles, not for other triangles.) Tj T*
(To find a shorter side, rearrange the formula: a^2 = c^2 - b^2.) Tj T*
(For a hypotenuse of 10 and a side of 6, the other side is 8.) Tj T*
(The distance between two points on a graph also uses the Pythagorean theorem.) Tj T*
(Sets of whole numbers like 3, 4, 5 are called Pythagorean triples.) Tj T*
(Students should always label the hypotenuse before using the formula.) Tj T*
(Remember to take the square root at the end to get the length of the side.) Tj T*
ET
